# FOPEN(3S)

## NAME

fopen, freopen, fdopen, open_memstream, tmpfile, tmpnam, remove, rename - open, name and remove
a stream's file

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <stdio.h>

FILE *fopen(const char *filename, const char *type);

FILE *freopen(const char *filename, const char *type, FILE *stream);

FILE *fdopen(int fildes, const char *type);

FILE *open_memstream(char **bufp, size_t *sizep);

FILE *tmpfile(void);

char *tmpnam(char *s);

int remove(const char *filename);

int rename(const char *from, const char *to);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Fopen* opens the file named by *filename* and associates a stream with it. *Fopen* returns a
pointer to be used to identify the stream in subsequent operations.

*Type* is a character string having one of the following values:

"r"
: open for reading

"w"
: create for writing

"a"
: append: open for writing at end of file, or create for writing

**Note:** a `+` may follow any of the three, opening the stream for update. **w+** and **a+**
are created and then reopened, because creat(2) leaves the descriptor write-only. A `b` is
accepted and ignored: there are no text and binary streams here, only bytes.

*Freopen* substitutes the named file in place of the open *stream*. It returns the original
value of *stream*. The original stream is closed.

*Freopen* is typically used to attach the preopened constant names, **stdin, stdout, stderr,**
to specified files.

*Fdopen* associates a stream with a file descriptor obtained from *open, dup, creat,* or
pipe(2). The *type* of the stream must agree with the mode of the open file.

*Open_memstream* opens a stream for writing whose sink is not a file but a buffer on the heap
that grows to hold whatever is written to it. It is POSIX.1-2008 and was in neither C11 nor v7.
The buffer and its length are reported through *bufp* and *sizep*, which POSIX says are valid
after each fflush(3) or fclose(3); here they are current at every moment, because the stream is
arranged so that the library sees each byte as it is written. The buffer is obtained from
malloc(3) and belongs to the caller, who must *free* it once the stream is closed.

The length in \**sizep* excludes the null byte that always terminates the buffer, so the result
may be read either as a string or as \**sizep* bytes. A null byte written to the stream is data
and is counted like any other.

**Note:** what this port does not honour. A memory stream carries no descriptor, so fseek(3),
*rewind* and ungetc(3) are not supported on one, and neither are setbuf(3) and *setvbuf,* which
would replace the very buffer the caller has been handed. Ftell(3) *is* supported and answers
the number of bytes written. Fileno(3) answers a number deliberately chosen to be no descriptor
at all, so that a stray write(2) or lseek(2) on a memory stream fails with EBADF rather than
reaching a real file.

**Note:** an allocation failure while the buffer is growing *loses* the text. This realloc(3)
frees a block before it allocates the new one and does not give it back on failure, so there is
nothing to fall back on; the loss is reported honestly as a null \**bufp*, a zero \**sizep* and
the error indication that ferror(3) reads. *Free* accepts the null, so a caller that frees
unconditionally is still correct.

**Note:** the four that follow are C11 and had no v7 ancestor.

*Tmpfile* creates a temporary file open for update and returns a stream on it. The name is
*unlinked* as soon as the stream holds the descriptor, so the file has no name from then on and
the kernel frees the inode when the last reference goes - which is either fclose(3) or the
cleanup that exit(3) runs. Nothing is left behind even if the program dies on a signal.

*Tmpnam* generates a file name that no existing file has, in a buffer of at least **L_tmpnam**
characters; a null *s* asks for a static buffer that the next call overwrites. It can
distinguish **TMP_MAX** names.

**Note:** *tmpnam* does *not* go through mktemp(3). That routine fills its trailing X's from
the process id and then walks a *single* letter, so it can distinguish 26 names where TMP_MAX
promises 26**3. *Tmpnam* counts three letters of its own, and the pid still separates one
process's names from another's.

*Remove* deletes a file; *rename* changes its name. Both return 0 on success and a non-zero
value on failure, and both are thin wrappers over unlink(2) and link(2)/unlink(2).

## SEE ALSO

open(2), fclose(3), setbuf(3), mktemp(3), stdio(3)

## DIAGNOSTICS

*Fopen, freopen, fdopen* and *tmpfile* return the pointer **NULL** if *filename* cannot be
accessed. *Open_memstream* returns NULL when every one of the FOPEN_MAX streams is already in
use, or when the initial buffer cannot be allocated. *Tmpnam* returns NULL if it cannot find an
unused name.

## BUGS

*Fdopen* is not portable to systems other than UNIX.

## BESM-6 NOTES

FOPEN_MAX is _NFILE, which is also NOFILE in <sys/param.h>: one **FILE** per descriptor and no
more.

**There is no path-length limit in this kernel.** *Namei*() walks a component at a time and
only the component is bounded, by DIRSIZ. So FILENAME_MAX is a convention for sizing a caller's
buffer and not a kernel number; it is v7's customary 128.

